  "asr_text": "If you hear \"cockroach milk\" and the cafe in your mind shuts down at once, I get it. But today, let's put the urgent meeting about whether to drink it on hold. The main story is not its taste or how it feels going down — it's the surprising way cockroach babies store food just for them. The Ig Nobel Prize went to research on nutrient-rich crystals that had been published earlier. This is not news that cockroach milk has finally made its debut at the dinner table. No one drank it as part of the study, and the study did not test its effects as a food for humans. You can put the cup back on the shelf. The study looked at a kind of cockroach that raises its young inside its body. The researchers took out protein crystals containing nutrients from the gut of the unborn young. You may have thought you were heading to the milk aisle, but instead you were shown the food store inside a body.",
